($bc11354$conflict-unfinished-business-13$bc11354$,$bc11355$large_gap_both_discontent$bc11355$,$bc11356$Case softened. Hurt receives a specific repair path, and effort receives a fair way to become visible.$bc11356$,$bc11357$[A] carries more remaining hurt; [B] feels more repaired.$bc11357$,$bc11358$[A] experiences the apology as too small for the impact. [B] experiences ongoing hurt as proof that nothing offered will ever count. Do not repeat the old argument while negotiating repair. Set two sessions: first identify the missing apology component, then review a concrete behavior period. If agreement on basic impact remains impossible, use neutral support before seeking final closure.$bc11358$,$bc11359$Prepare the impact and one missing component for the first session, without expanding into unrelated history. Prepare the responsibility you can own and the behavior you will sustain through the review period.$bc11359$,$bc11360$Case softened. Hurt receives a specific repair path, and effort receives a fair way to become visible.$bc11360$,$bc11361$Approved v4 template$bc11361$,$bc11362$Rename$bc11362$,$bc11363$[A] carries more remaining hurt; [B] feels more repaired.$bc11363$,$bc11364$Prepare the impact and one missing component for the first session, without expanding into unrelated history.$bc11364$,$bc11365$Prepare the responsibility you can own and the behavior you will sustain through the review period.$bc11365$,$bc11366$Both of you save a Two-Session Apology Completion plan with impact, missing component, behavior period, evidence, and support threshold.$bc11366$,$bc11367$Case softened. Hurt receives a specific repair path, and effort receives a fair way to become visible.$bc11367$),
($bc11368$conflict-unfinished-business-14$bc11368$,$bc11369$fun_overlap$bc11369$,$bc11370$Case softened. Frustration gets a voice before it needs to keep borrowing attitude.$bc11370$,$bc11371$$bc11371$,$bc11372$You recognize the same unmet need and the frustration is still manageable. That is good timing for a clean request before irritation recruits unrelated evidence. Name one current example, the need underneath it, and one behavior that would help this week. The listener should respond to the request, not investigate every sign that appeared before it was spoken.$bc11372$,$bc11373$Turn the frustration into one direct request with a clear time frame. Respond to the request and complete one visible action without demanding a perfect explanation of the delay.$bc11373$,$bc11374$Case softened. Frustration gets a voice before it needs to keep borrowing attitude.$bc11374$,$bc11375$Approved v4 template$bc11375$,$bc11376$Root Swap$bc11376$,$bc11377$$bc11377$,$bc11378$Turn the frustration into one direct request with a clear time frame.$bc11378$,$bc11379$Respond to the request and complete one visible action without demanding a perfect explanation of the delay.$bc11379$,$bc11380$Both of you save a Quiet-to-Clear Request with example, need, behavior, owner, time frame, and follow-up.$bc11380$,$bc11381$Case softened. Frustration gets a voice before it needs to keep borrowing attitude.$bc11381$),
($bc11382$conflict-unfinished-business-14$bc11382$,$bc11383$same_territory$bc11383$,$bc11384$Case softened. The relationship receives help for today and evidence for the weeks after it.$bc11384$,$bc11385$$bc11385$,$bc11386$You agree on the need, but the frustration now carries real weight because silence has also created distance. The repair has two parts: address the unmet need and acknowledge how the quiet leak affected the atmosphere. Choose one immediate relief action and one sustained habit. A single kind gesture cannot replace a pattern, but it can make room to build one.$bc11386$,$bc11387$Name the accumulated cost and the immediate relief action that would help you reenter connection. Complete that action, then own a repeatable habit with a date for reviewing whether it changed the pattern.$bc11387$,$bc11388$Case softened. The relationship receives help for today and evidence for the weeks after it.$bc11388$,$bc11389$Approved v4 template$bc11389$,$bc11390$Shared Discovery$bc11390$,$bc11391$$bc11391$,$bc11392$Name the accumulated cost and the immediate relief action that would help you reenter connection.$bc11392$,$bc11393$Complete that action, then own a repeatable habit with a date for reviewing whether it changed the pattern.$bc11393$,$bc11394$Both of you save an Immediate and Ongoing Frustration Repair with impact, relief action, habit, cue, and review.$bc11394$,$bc11395$Case softened. The relationship receives help for today and evidence for the weeks after it.$bc11395$),
($bc11396$conflict-unfinished-business-14$bc11396$,$bc11397$two_different_facts$bc11397$,$bc11398$Case softened. Two quiet frustrations become two clear chances to show up.$bc11398$,$bc11399$[A] and [B] selected different unmet needs with manageable emotional weight.$bc11399$,$bc11400$[A]'s quiet frustration is asking for more support, follow-through, or attention. [B]'s is asking for more space, initiative, respect, or another distinct need. These requests may look like opposites, but each points to a different depleted lane. Make one change for each lane and avoid trading them as conditions. Neither need has to lose for the other to become visible.$bc11400$,$bc11401$State your need as one behavior [B] can complete without guessing. State your different need and choose one action you can take for [A] before the review.$bc11401$,$bc11402$Case softened. Two quiet frustrations become two clear chances to show up.$bc11402$,$bc11403$Approved v4 template$bc11403$,$bc11404$Reframe$bc11404$,$bc11405$[A] and [B] selected different unmet needs with manageable emotional weight.$bc11405$,$bc11406$State your need as one behavior [B] can complete without guessing.$bc11406$,$bc11407$State your different need and choose one action you can take for [A] before the review.$bc11407$,$bc11408$Both of you save a Two-Lane Frustration Reset with needs, behaviors, owners, start dates, and shared review.$bc11408$,$bc11409$Case softened. Two quiet frustrations become two clear chances to show up.$bc11409$),
($bc11410$conflict-unfinished-business-14$bc11410$,$bc11411$making_room_for_both$bc11411$,$bc11412$Case softened. Both warning lights receive attention before either becomes the whole dashboard.$bc11412$,$bc11413$[A] and [B] selected different unmet needs with high emotional weight.$bc11413$,$bc11414$[A] and [B] carry different frustrations, and both have grown heavy enough to affect warmth or effort. Solving only the louder one will teach the quieter partner to store more. Use parallel first aid: one immediate action for each need, followed by separate habit plans. Review progress together, but do not make either repair contingent on gratitude for the other.$bc11414$,$bc11415$Choose the immediate action that would reduce [B]'s current strain and schedule it now. Choose a separate action for [A], then build the longer habit attached to your own responsibility.$bc11415$,$bc11416$Case softened. Both warning lights receive attention before either becomes the whole dashboard.$bc11416$,$bc11417$Approved v4 template$bc11417$,$bc11418$Unexpected Analogy$bc11418$,$bc11419$[A] and [B] selected different unmet needs with high emotional weight.$bc11419$,$bc11420$Choose the immediate action that would reduce [B]'s current strain and schedule it now.$bc11420$,$bc11421$Choose a separate action for [A], then build the longer habit attached to your own responsibility.$bc11421$,$bc11422$Both of you save a Parallel Frustration First-Aid plan with two actions, two habits, cues, owners, and review date.$bc11422$,$bc11423$Case softened. Both warning lights receive attention before either becomes the whole dashboard.$bc11423$),
($bc11424$conflict-unfinished-business-15$bc11424$,$bc11425$consensus_a$bc11425$,$bc11426$Case softened. An old agreement gets a current decision instead of indefinite imaginary progress.$bc11426$,$bc11427$[A] = the partner both identified as more likely to leave the agreement unstarted.$bc11427$,$bc11428$[A] is more likely to leave the agreement in conversation because shared enthusiasm can feel like shared execution. [B] may wait for the promised movement and read silence as low priority. [A] needs to own the first visible step or state that commitment has changed. [B] needs to confirm the plan is still mutually chosen before enforcing an old yes.$bc11428$,$bc11429$Confirm whether you still choose the agreement, then own and schedule the first step. State the impact of the stall and ask for a fresh yes, redesign, or honest no.$bc11429$,$bc11430$Case softened. An old agreement gets a current decision instead of indefinite imaginary progress.$bc11430$,$bc11431$Approved v4 template$bc11431$,$bc11432$Reframe$bc11432$,$bc11433$[A] = the partner both identified as more likely to leave the agreement unstarted.$bc11433$,$bc11434$Confirm whether you still choose the agreement, then own and schedule the first step.$bc11434$,$bc11435$State the impact of the stall and ask for a fresh yes, redesign, or honest no.$bc11435$,$bc11436$Both of you save an Agreement Restart Sheet with current yes, first step, owners, resources, date, and redesign option.$bc11436$,$bc11437$Case softened. An old agreement gets a current decision instead of indefinite imaginary progress.$bc11437$),
($bc11438$conflict-unfinished-business-15$bc11438$,$bc11439$consensus_b$bc11439$,$bc11440$Case softened. The plan receives a real start or an honest release from pretending.$bc11440$,$bc11441$[B] = the partner both identified as more likely to leave the agreement unstarted.$bc11441$,$bc11442$[B] treats agreement as enough to create momentum, while [A] experiences a plan that never leaves the meeting. [B] should either schedule the first action or withdraw the commitment honestly. [A] should not keep carrying a plan that may only have received a conflict-ending yes. A fresh agreement needs owners and resources before it deserves another deadline.$bc11442$,$bc11443$Ask whether the plan is still genuinely chosen and identify the consequence of continued delay. Give a current answer, then schedule the first action or clearly propose a redesign.$bc11443$,$bc11444$Case softened. The plan receives a real start or an honest release from pretending.$bc11444$,$bc11445$Approved v4 template$bc11445$,$bc11446$Unexpected Analogy$bc11446$,$bc11447$[B] = the partner both identified as more likely to leave the agreement unstarted.$bc11447$,$bc11448$Ask whether the plan is still genuinely chosen and identify the consequence of continued delay.$bc11448$,$bc11449$Give a current answer, then schedule the first action or clearly propose a redesign.$bc11449$,$bc11450$Both of you save an Agreement Restart Sheet with current yes, first step, owners, resources, date, and redesign option.$bc11450$,$bc11451$Case softened. The plan receives a real start or an honest release from pretending.$bc11451$),
($bc11452$conflict-unfinished-business-15$bc11452$,$bc11453$consensus_both$bc11453$,$bc11454$Case softened. Shared intention becomes action only where both people still choose the plan.$bc11454$,$bc11455$$bc11455$,$bc11456$Both of you agree easily and start slowly, which suggests the relationship is good at shared intention but vague about execution. Before making another plan, close the old loop. Confirm desire, assign the first action to one person, set the date, and identify the resource. If no one wants the first step, the agreement needs redesign rather than motivation.$bc11456$,$bc11457$Choose one stalled agreement and either claim the first step or say which part you no longer choose. Choose the next dependent step and arrange the resource it needs before the start date.$bc11457$,$bc11458$Case softened. Shared intention becomes action only where both people still choose the plan.$bc11458$,$bc11459$Approved v4 template$bc11459$,$bc11460$Rename$bc11460$,$bc11461$$bc11461$,$bc11462$Choose one stalled agreement and either claim the first step or say which part you no longer choose.$bc11462$,$bc11463$Choose the next dependent step and arrange the resource it needs before the start date.$bc11463$,$bc11464$Both of you save a Stalled Agreement Triage with keep, redesign, release, first step, owner, resource, and date.$bc11464$,$bc11465$Case softened. Shared intention becomes action only where both people still choose the plan.$bc11465$),
($bc11466$conflict-unfinished-business-15$bc11466$,$bc11467$self_claim_clash$bc11467$,$bc11468$Case softened. The stalled plan discovers which action actually has to move first.$bc11468$,$bc11469$$bc11469$,$bc11470$Each of you sees yourself as the one who fails to start, but different agreements may be waiting on different owners. Map the dependency chain. [A]'s action may need to happen before [B] can move, or both may be waiting for a joint resource. Identify the true first domino instead of dividing vague responsibility equally.$bc11470$,$bc11471$Name the earliest action you control and complete it before requesting the next step from [B]. Name the action that depends on [A]'s step and prepare everything you can in advance.$bc11471$,$bc11472$Case softened. The stalled plan discovers which action actually has to move first.$bc11472$,$bc11473$Approved v4 template$bc11473$,$bc11474$Shared Discovery$bc11474$,$bc11475$$bc11475$,$bc11476$Name the earliest action you control and complete it before requesting the next step from [B].$bc11476$,$bc11477$Name the action that depends on [A]'s step and prepare everything you can in advance.$bc11477$,$bc11478$Both of you save a First-Domino Plan with dependency order, owners, resources, dates, and completion marks.$bc11478$,$bc11479$Case softened. The stalled plan discovers which action actually has to move first.$bc11479$),
($bc11480$conflict-unfinished-business-15$bc11480$,$bc11481$mutual_deflection$bc11481$,$bc11482$Case softened. Shared plans stop disappearing into the space between two responsible people.$bc11482$,$bc11483$$bc11483$,$bc11484$Each of you thinks the other usually leaves agreements untouched, which means shared ownership may be functioning as ownerless work. Audit the three active agreements. Give every first step one name, not both names. Remove anything neither person currently chooses. A smaller list with clear owners will reveal more than another argument about initiative.$bc11484$,$bc11485$Claim one first step and release one agreement you no longer genuinely support. Claim a different step and state the date by which you will report completion or an obstacle.$bc11485$,$bc11486$Case softened. Shared plans stop disappearing into the space between two responsible people.$bc11486$,$bc11487$Approved v4 template$bc11487$,$bc11488$Root Swap$bc11488$,$bc11489$$bc11489$,$bc11490$Claim one first step and release one agreement you no longer genuinely support.$bc11490$,$bc11491$Claim a different step and state the date by which you will report completion or an obstacle.$bc11491$,$bc11492$Both of you save a Three-Agreement Ownership Board with keep or release status, first steps, single owners, dates, and obstacles.$bc11492$,$bc11493$Case softened. Shared plans stop disappearing into the space between two responsible people.$bc11493$),
($bc11494$conflict-unfinished-business-15$bc11494$,$bc11495$partial_overlap$bc11495$,$bc11496$Case softened. One old plan gets movement without hiding responsibility inside the word 'we.'$bc11496$,$bc11497$[A] = identified a specific more frequent nonstarter; [B] = said the pattern is shared or situational.$bc11497$,$bc11498$[A] identifies one partner as the more frequent nonstarter while [B] sees the pattern as shared or situational. Start with the longest-stalled agreement and make ownership explicit. The nonstarter takes the first action. The other partner prepares the next dependency and does not rescue the first step. Review only after the agreed date.$bc11498$,$bc11499$Complete the first action without waiting for reminders and report any real obstacle before the date. Prepare the dependent action and let the first owner carry their step fully.$bc11499$,$bc11500$Case softened. One old plan gets movement without hiding responsibility inside the word 'we.'$bc11500$,$bc11501$Approved v4 template$bc11501$,$bc11502$Reframe$bc11502$,$bc11503$[A] = identified a specific more frequent nonstarter; [B] = said the pattern is shared or situational.$bc11503$,$bc11504$Complete the first action without waiting for reminders and report any real obstacle before the date.$bc11504$,$bc11505$Prepare the dependent action and let the first owner carry their step fully.$bc11505$,$bc11506$Both of you save a Longest-Stalled Restart with current commitment, first owner, next dependency, dates, obstacle rule, and review.$bc11506$,$bc11507$Case softened. One old plan gets movement without hiding responsibility inside the word 'we.'$bc11507$),
($bc11508$conflict-unfinished-business-16$bc11508$,$bc11509$small_gap_both_content$bc11509$,$bc11510$Case softened. The agreement stays strong because it is allowed to remain current.$bc11510$,$bc11511$$bc11511$,$bc11512$You see the old compromise similarly and it still fits well enough because its purpose remains relevant and its costs remain visible. That is not permission to leave it untouched forever. Name what has changed and schedule a future review. A fair compromise stays fair partly because both people are allowed to update the data without threatening the original goodwill.$bc11512$,$bc11513$Name the current benefit you still receive and the change that should trigger an earlier review. Name the cost you still accept and the limit that would make the arrangement need revision.$bc11513$,$bc11514$Case softened. The agreement stays strong because it is allowed to remain current.$bc11514$,$bc11515$Approved v4 template$bc11515$,$bc11516$Root Swap$bc11516$,$bc11517$$bc11517$,$bc11518$Name the current benefit you still receive and the change that should trigger an earlier review.$bc11518$,$bc11519$Name the cost you still accept and the limit that would make the arrangement need revision.$bc11519$,$bc11520$Both of you save a Living Compromise Record with original purpose, current benefits, current costs, trigger points, and review date.$bc11520$,$bc11521$Case softened. The agreement stays strong because it is allowed to remain current.$bc11521$),
($bc11522$conflict-unfinished-business-16$bc11522$,$bc11523$small_gap_split$bc11523$,$bc11524$Case softened. A small update protects the bargain from becoming quietly outdated.$bc11524$,$bc11525$[A] = less content with the current compromise; [B] = more content.$bc11525$,$bc11526$[A] and [B] see a similar fit, but [A] is less content because a small update has become overdue. [B] sees the original exchange still functioning. [A] feels a changed schedule, energy level, or responsibility making one side more expensive. Preserve the purpose and adjust the part whose real cost changed.$bc11526$,$bc11527$Identify the updated cost and request one proportional shift rather than discarding the whole compromise. Accept the cost update and take or fund the adjustment for a defined trial period.$bc11527$,$bc11528$Case softened. A small update protects the bargain from becoming quietly outdated.$bc11528$,$bc11529$Approved v4 template$bc11529$,$bc11530$Shared Discovery$bc11530$,$bc11531$[A] = less content with the current compromise; [B] = more content.$bc11531$,$bc11532$Identify the updated cost and request one proportional shift rather than discarding the whole compromise.$bc11532$,$bc11533$Accept the cost update and take or fund the adjustment for a defined trial period.$bc11533$,$bc11534$Both of you save a Compromise Tune-Up with original purpose, changed cost, specific shift, trial dates, and review.$bc11534$,$bc11535$Case softened. A small update protects the bargain from becoming quietly outdated.$bc11535$),
($bc11536$conflict-unfinished-business-16$bc11536$,$bc11537$small_gap_both_discontent$bc11537$,$bc11538$Case softened. The earlier compromise can be appreciated without being forced to govern a different life.$bc11538$,$bc11539$$bc11539$,$bc11540$You agree the compromise no longer fits comfortably, but may be preserving it because changing it feels like undoing earlier generosity. The original agreement solved an older life. Honor that purpose, then retire the obsolete mechanics. Recalculate today's time, money, energy, and alternatives. Choose a three-month version with an automatic review instead of another permanent promise.$bc11540$,$bc11541$List the cost you carry now and the part of the old arrangement you are ready to release. List your current cost and design the three-month shift you can sustain without hidden dependence.$bc11541$,$bc11542$Case softened. The earlier compromise can be appreciated without being forced to govern a different life.$bc11542$,$bc11543$Approved v4 template$bc11543$,$bc11544$Unexpected Analogy$bc11544$,$bc11545$$bc11545$,$bc11546$List the cost you carry now and the part of the old arrangement you are ready to release.$bc11546$,$bc11547$List your current cost and design the three-month shift you can sustain without hidden dependence.$bc11547$,$bc11548$Both of you save a Three-Month Compromise Update with old purpose, current costs, new split, start date, and automatic review.$bc11548$,$bc11549$Case softened. The earlier compromise can be appreciated without being forced to govern a different life.$bc11549$),
($bc11550$conflict-unfinished-business-16$bc11550$,$bc11551$large_gap_both_content$bc11551$,$bc11552$Case softened. Continuity stays, but it no longer asks one partner to absorb the whole update in circumstances.$bc11552$,$bc11553$[A] experiences the compromise as less fair; [B] sees more remaining fit.$bc11553$,$bc11554$[A] experiences the compromise as much less fair because circumstances changed the cost on [A]'s side. [B] still experiences it as workable and may value the stability it provides. You are content because stability can remain while the unequal cost is offset. Keep the original structure temporarily, but transfer one responsibility, resource, or freedom block to rebalance it.$bc11554$,$bc11555$Quantify the changed cost and choose the offset that would make the next month sustainable. Provide that offset fully and help collect the data for the scheduled redesign review.$bc11555$,$bc11556$Case softened. Continuity stays, but it no longer asks one partner to absorb the whole update in circumstances.$bc11556$,$bc11557$Approved v4 template$bc11557$,$bc11558$Reframe$bc11558$,$bc11559$[A] experiences the compromise as less fair; [B] sees more remaining fit.$bc11559$,$bc11560$Quantify the changed cost and choose the offset that would make the next month sustainable.$bc11560$,$bc11561$Provide that offset fully and help collect the data for the scheduled redesign review.$bc11561$,$bc11562$Both of you save a Stability With Offset plan with current structure, changed cost, offset, owner, trial month, and redesign date.$bc11562$,$bc11563$Case softened. Continuity stays, but it no longer asks one partner to absorb the whole update in circumstances.$bc11563$),
($bc11564$conflict-unfinished-business-16$bc11564$,$bc11565$large_gap_split$bc11565$,$bc11566$Case softened. The old agreement receives an orderly update instead of permanent authority.$bc11566$,$bc11567$[A] experiences the compromise as less fair and is dissatisfied; [B] sees more fit and is content.$bc11567$,$bc11568$[A] experiences the old compromise as deeply unfair and is dissatisfied. [B] still benefits from or trusts the arrangement and feels content. The agreement's history cannot make today's unequal cost automatic. [B] owes one immediate offset and a redesign date. [A] owes a concrete cost account and a bridge proposal rather than withdrawing without transition.$bc11568$,$bc11569$Document the current cost and propose the shortest bridge that protects essential commitments. Take the immediate offset, agree to the redesign date, and stop treating the original consent as permanent consent.$bc11569$,$bc11570$Case softened. The old agreement receives an orderly update instead of permanent authority.$bc11570$,$bc11571$Approved v4 template$bc11571$,$bc11572$Rename$bc11572$,$bc11573$[A] experiences the compromise as less fair and is dissatisfied; [B] sees more fit and is content.$bc11573$,$bc11574$Document the current cost and propose the shortest bridge that protects essential commitments.$bc11574$,$bc11575$Take the immediate offset, agree to the redesign date, and stop treating the original consent as permanent consent.$bc11575$,$bc11576$Both of you save a Compromise Exit Bridge with current cost, immediate offset, protected commitments, bridge length, and redesign date.$bc11576$,$bc11577$Case softened. The old agreement receives an orderly update instead of permanent authority.$bc11577$),
($bc11578$conflict-unfinished-business-16$bc11578$,$bc11579$large_gap_both_discontent$bc11579$,$bc11580$Case softened. Fairness gets a date, and stability gets a bridge instead of a veto.$bc11580$,$bc11581$[A] experiences the compromise as less fair; [B] sees more remaining fit.$bc11581$,$bc11582$[A] sees an arrangement that now extracts too much. [B] sees the risk of losing stability if it changes quickly. Do not choose between continued unfairness and sudden collapse. Set an end date for the current terms, create a temporary bridge, and design the next arrangement from current capacity. A dated transition makes change safer than indefinite resentment.$bc11582$,$bc11583$Name the nonnegotiable end date and the essential commitments the bridge must protect. Name the stability need and take responsibility for one transition task before the old terms expire.$bc11583$,$bc11584$Case softened. Fairness gets a date, and stability gets a bridge instead of a veto.$bc11584$,$bc11585$Approved v4 template$bc11585$,$bc11586$Root Swap$bc11586$,$bc11587$[A] experiences the compromise as less fair; [B] sees more remaining fit.$bc11587$,$bc11588$Name the nonnegotiable end date and the essential commitments the bridge must protect.$bc11588$,$bc11589$Name the stability need and take responsibility for one transition task before the old terms expire.$bc11589$,$bc11590$Both of you save a Dated Compromise Transition with end date, bridge terms, protected needs, transition owners, and new-plan meeting.$bc11590$,$bc11591$Case softened. Fairness gets a date, and stability gets a bridge instead of a veto.$bc11591$),
($bc11592$conflict-unfinished-business-17$bc11592$,$bc11593$fun_overlap$bc11593$,$bc11594$Case softened. The unfinished issue gets a first chapter small enough to tell accurately.$bc11594$,$bc11595$$bc11595$,$bc11596$You agree on what blocked the trust conversation and its current weight is manageable enough for a paced start. Do not attempt the whole history at once. Exchange short timelines, identify one fact question, and separate it from the impact discussion. Trust repairs better when clarity and emotion each receive a turn instead of competing inside every sentence.$bc11596$,$bc11597$Write the timeline as you remember it and mark the one question whose answer matters most now. Write a separate timeline, identify any uncertainty honestly, and prepare to reflect the impact before explaining intent.$bc11597$,$bc11598$Case softened. The unfinished issue gets a first chapter small enough to tell accurately.$bc11598$,$bc11599$Approved v4 template$bc11599$,$bc11600$Unexpected Analogy$bc11600$,$bc11601$$bc11601$,$bc11602$Write the timeline as you remember it and mark the one question whose answer matters most now.$bc11602$,$bc11603$Write a separate timeline, identify any uncertainty honestly, and prepare to reflect the impact before explaining intent.$bc11603$,$bc11604$Both of you save a Trust Talk Starter with two timelines, one fact question, impact turn, pause rule, and next date.$bc11604$,$bc11605$Case softened. The unfinished issue gets a first chapter small enough to tell accurately.$bc11605$),
($bc11606$conflict-unfinished-business-17$bc11606$,$bc11607$same_territory$bc11607$,$bc11608$Case softened. The issue receives enough structure that honesty does not have to survive chaos to be heard.$bc11608$,$bc11609$$bc11609$,$bc11610$You share the reason the topic stayed unfinished, but it now carries heavy weight and shapes checking, intimacy, or belief. This needs more than an improvised late-night conversation. Schedule two sessions and preselect support. The first establishes facts and unknowns. The second addresses impact, responsibility, and repair. Either person can call the agreed pause without canceling the process.$bc11610$,$bc11611$Prepare your timeline, known facts, unknowns, and the impact you need acknowledged. Prepare the same structure and identify the responsibility you can discuss without waiting for perfect agreement on every detail.$bc11611$,$bc11612$Case softened. The issue receives enough structure that honesty does not have to survive chaos to be heard.$bc11612$,$bc11613$Approved v4 template$bc11613$,$bc11614$Reframe$bc11614$,$bc11615$$bc11615$,$bc11616$Prepare your timeline, known facts, unknowns, and the impact you need acknowledged.$bc11616$,$bc11617$Prepare the same structure and identify the responsibility you can discuss without waiting for perfect agreement on every detail.$bc11617$,$bc11618$Both of you save a Two-Session Trust Process with agendas, dates, pause rule, knowns, unknowns, repair question, and support contact.$bc11618$,$bc11619$Case softened. The issue receives enough structure that honesty does not have to survive chaos to be heard.$bc11619$),
($bc11620$conflict-unfinished-business-17$bc11620$,$bc11621$two_different_facts$bc11621$,$bc11622$Case softened. Two different barriers become instructions for a conversation that can actually begin.$bc11622$,$bc11623$[A] and [B] chose different barriers to the trust conversation with manageable emotional weight.$bc11623$,$bc11624$[A] avoided the trust talk because the facts or reaction felt dangerous. [B] avoided it because privacy, uncertainty, or a major decision was at stake. One barrier needs emotional safety. The other needs scope control. Begin with written boundaries around what the first session will cover, what remains private, and which decision is explicitly not being made yet.$bc11624$,$bc11625$Name the safety condition and first question you need in order to stay present. Name the privacy or decision boundary and the information you can still share inside it.$bc11625$,$bc11626$Case softened. Two different barriers become instructions for a conversation that can actually begin.$bc11626$,$bc11627$Approved v4 template$bc11627$,$bc11628$Root Swap$bc11628$,$bc11629$[A] and [B] chose different barriers to the trust conversation with manageable emotional weight.$bc11629$,$bc11630$Name the safety condition and first question you need in order to stay present.$bc11630$,$bc11631$Name the privacy or decision boundary and the information you can still share inside it.$bc11631$,$bc11632$Both of you save a Safe-Scope Trust Agenda with first question, safety rules, privacy line, deferred decisions, and next step.$bc11632$,$bc11633$Case softened. Two different barriers become instructions for a conversation that can actually begin.$bc11633$),
($bc11634$conflict-unfinished-business-17$bc11634$,$bc11635$making_room_for_both$bc11635$,$bc11636$Case softened. The relationship gets a route through the issue without asking either person to carry the whole route alone.$bc11636$,$bc11637$[A] and [B] chose different barriers to the trust conversation with high emotional weight.$bc11637$,$bc11638$[A] and [B] carry different heavy barriers, and the unfinished trust issue already affects daily closeness. Trying to solve facts, impact, privacy, and future decisions in one talk will overwhelm the process. Use a counselor or mediator and divide the work into four meetings: scope, facts, impact, and repair choices. Safety exceptions remain active throughout.$bc11638$,$bc11639$Prepare a one-page scope note with what you can discuss, what support you need, and the first factual question. Prepare a separate note with privacy limits, known information, and the repair outcome you hope the process can reach.$bc11639$,$bc11640$Case softened. The relationship gets a route through the issue without asking either person to carry the whole route alone.$bc11640$,$bc11641$Approved v4 template$bc11641$,$bc11642$Shared Discovery$bc11642$,$bc11643$[A] and [B] chose different barriers to the trust conversation with high emotional weight.$bc11643$,$bc11644$Prepare a one-page scope note with what you can discuss, what support you need, and the first factual question.$bc11644$,$bc11645$Prepare a separate note with privacy limits, known information, and the repair outcome you hope the process can reach.$bc11645$,$bc11646$Both of you save a Four-Meeting Trust Roadmap with scope, facts, impact, repair sessions, facilitator, and safety exceptions.$bc11646$,$bc11647$Case softened. The relationship gets a route through the issue without asking either person to carry the whole route alone.$bc11647$),
($bc11648$conflict-unfinished-business-18$bc11648$,$bc11649$fun_overlap$bc11649$,$bc11650$Case softened. The issue gets a visible place to return to instead of another polite disappearance.$bc11650$,$bc11651$$bc11651$,$bc11652$You agree on what keeps postponing the issue and its current weight is still manageable. The next risk is not lack of insight. It is another vague promise to revisit it. Name the issue in one sentence, choose the first decision or repair, and put the talk on the calendar. A continuation note keeps the subject from disappearing when the mood improves.$bc11652$,$bc11653$Write the one-sentence issue and the first outcome you need from the scheduled talk. Choose the date and prepare the information or reflection needed for that first outcome.$bc11653$,$bc11654$Case softened. The issue gets a visible place to return to instead of another polite disappearance.$bc11654$,$bc11655$Approved v4 template$bc11655$,$bc11656$Shared Discovery$bc11656$,$bc11657$$bc11657$,$bc11658$Write the one-sentence issue and the first outcome you need from the scheduled talk.$bc11658$,$bc11659$Choose the date and prepare the information or reflection needed for that first outcome.$bc11659$,$bc11660$Both of you save a No-More-Rug Continuation Note with issue, current impact, first outcome, preparation owners, and meeting date.$bc11660$,$bc11661$Case softened. The issue gets a visible place to return to instead of another polite disappearance.$bc11661$),
($bc11662$conflict-unfinished-business-18$bc11662$,$bc11663$same_territory$bc11663$,$bc11664$Case softened. Reliability begins repairing what repeated postponement added to the original issue.$bc11664$,$bc11665$$bc11665$,$bc11666$You share the postponement pattern, but the issue now carries serious weight because avoidance has become part of the injury. The first repair is reliability: schedule the conversation, keep it, and finish one defined task. Do not promise to solve everything. Proving that the issue can stay on the table long enough for one outcome matters more than an ambitious agenda.$bc11666$,$bc11667$Define the single decision or acknowledgment the first meeting must complete. Book the meeting, own the preparation task, and protect the time from lower-priority interruptions.$bc11667$,$bc11668$Case softened. Reliability begins repairing what repeated postponement added to the original issue.$bc11668$,$bc11669$Approved v4 template$bc11669$,$bc11670$Root Swap$bc11670$,$bc11671$$bc11671$,$bc11672$Define the single decision or acknowledgment the first meeting must complete.$bc11672$,$bc11673$Book the meeting, own the preparation task, and protect the time from lower-priority interruptions.$bc11673$,$bc11674$Both of you save a Keep-the-Date Repair with issue, avoidance impact, first task, preparation, meeting date, and follow-up.$bc11674$,$bc11675$Case softened. Reliability begins repairing what repeated postponement added to the original issue.$bc11675$),
($bc11676$conflict-unfinished-business-18$bc11676$,$bc11677$two_different_facts$bc11677$,$bc11678$Case softened. Timing and scope stop taking turns as reasons the issue cannot begin.$bc11678$,$bc11679$[A] and [B] selected different reasons the issue is postponed with manageable emotional weight.$bc11679$,$bc11680$[A] sees the issue buried because calm feels fragile or timing is repeatedly denied. [B] sees it buried because the topic is vague, sprawling, or displaced by urgent problems. One partner needs commitment to return. The other needs a smaller scope. Set the date first, then narrow the meeting to one question that can actually be completed.$bc11680$,$bc11681$Name the cost of postponement and the date by which the first talk needs to happen. Turn the broad issue into one answerable question and list what is outside the first meeting.$bc11681$,$bc11682$Case softened. Timing and scope stop taking turns as reasons the issue cannot begin.$bc11682$,$bc11683$Approved v4 template$bc11683$,$bc11684$Rename$bc11684$,$bc11685$[A] and [B] selected different reasons the issue is postponed with manageable emotional weight.$bc11685$,$bc11686$Name the cost of postponement and the date by which the first talk needs to happen.$bc11686$,$bc11687$Turn the broad issue into one answerable question and list what is outside the first meeting.$bc11687$,$bc11688$Both of you save a Date and Scope Agreement with postponement cost, meeting date, first question, excluded topics, and follow-up.$bc11688$,$bc11689$Case softened. Timing and scope stop taking turns as reasons the issue cannot begin.$bc11689$),
($bc11690$conflict-unfinished-business-18$bc11690$,$bc11691$making_room_for_both$bc11691$,$bc11692$Case softened. The issue may move slowly, but it no longer gets to vanish without a next step.$bc11692$,$bc11693$[A] and [B] selected different reasons the issue is postponed with high emotional weight.$bc11693$,$bc11694$[A] experiences repeated postponement as evidence the issue will never matter enough. [B] experiences the topic as too large or destabilizing to approach safely. Both concerns now carry heavy weight. Use a firm date, written summaries, timed turns, and a preselected support option. If the first meeting stalls, the support appointment activates rather than the issue disappearing again.$bc11694$,$bc11695$Write the impact of postponement and the one outcome you need from the first meeting. Write the scope and safety rules you need, then arrange the backup support before the meeting date.$bc11695$,$bc11696$Case softened. The issue may move slowly, but it no longer gets to vanish without a next step.$bc11696$,$bc11697$Approved v4 template$bc11697$,$bc11698$Reframe$bc11698$,$bc11699$[A] and [B] selected different reasons the issue is postponed with high emotional weight.$bc11699$,$bc11700$Write the impact of postponement and the one outcome you need from the first meeting.$bc11700$,$bc11701$Write the scope and safety rules you need, then arrange the backup support before the meeting date.$bc11701$,$bc11702$Both of you save an Anti-Avoidance Escalation Plan with summaries, firm date, timed turns, first outcome, and automatic support step.$bc11702$,$bc11703$Case softened. The issue may move slowly, but it no longer gets to vanish without a next step.$bc11703$);
